emaining 20\". Hmm, that's another inconsistency: $20 spread over 21 days = $0.95, while the sheet says 20 days remaining. If it should spread over the 20 remaining days → $1.00. This is a clearer \"wrong_value\" bug maybe. But \"spread over what's left of the period\" includes today? If it's for today, there are 21 days including today. The sheet's \"20 days remaining\" might exclude today. So ambiguous.\n\nHmm, what about the ProgressBar 0.1566? Maybe it's the percentage of the period elapsed: 4/24 = 0.1667 not 0.1566. Hmm, 0.15666 ≈ 3.76/24. Could be the \"spent/budget\" = 0.15666? That would mean spent $15.67, not $80. Hmm! Maybe the circular progress uses a different spent: maybe available? 15.67% ~ available 20%? no.\n\nMaybe the ProgressBar value 0.1566 is the *daily average spent*? Hmm, no.\n\nLet's not go there.\n\nLet me consider that the budget sheet's \"Spent\" might be intended to include the recurring template's \"next payment\" that is upcoming within the period. But the period ends Dec 4 and rent next would be Dec 13 — outside. Hmm. Unless the recurring template's next occurrence is Nov 13 (already paid) and the app counts it twice due to a logic bug. That's the seeded bug.\n\nI'll file again, maybe with clearer focus on the *inconsistency* and the recurring template. Let me use observed_quote \"Spent $80\" — actually the quote should be exact text. The card shows \"$80\" and \"Spent\" separately. I could quote \"$80\" and \"Available: 20%\". Let me quote \"$80\" and mention \"Available: 20%\".\n\nActually maybe I should test the scenario differently: Disable the recurring template / delete the recurring rent template and see if spent drops by 20. But deleting the template may be too destructive. Is there a way to view/manage subscriptions? Maybe in Analytics → \"Past periods\"? Hmm. There might be a \"subscriptions\" screen accessible via Settings. Let me look at the Settings again: \"Recurring payments time\" under Notifications. Not management.\n\nMaybe on the history screen, the \"Rent\" entry with \"Recurrent expense\" can be tapped → detail → maybe has a \"Stop recurring\" option. If I tap the Rent entry and there's \"Delete series\" vs \"Delete this\", that would tell. But deleting the template would change data — we could re-add though. Risky.\n\nLet me instead check the second budget sheet row: \"Spent $80\" might include the recurring template because the template is an \"entry\" in the summary. The dev couldn't reproduce possibly because they tested on a different build or the seeded bug differs.\n\nHmm, \"could not reproduce with the steps given\" from a developer: maybe it means they responded to my claim but couldn't repro; I should try to find the *actual* reproducible defect. Maybe the actual bug is that the *Analytics* total is wrong (should be $80), not the budget sheet. Which one is \"right\" per spec? The precondition says: budget of 100, yesterday one-off Groceries 40 and recurring Rent 20. Total expenses = 60. So spent should be 60. Analytics is right; budget sheet wrong. But maybe the app counts \"recurring monthly Rent 20\" twice legitimately because there are two periods? No.\n\nWait wait. Precondition says \"Yesterday there was a one-off Groceries expense of 40.00 (Food) and a recurring monthly Rent expense of 20.00 (Food).\" Could mean there was a recurring monthly rent expense of $20 (so the transaction is part of the recurring series). Yes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the budget sheet includes the recurring template as a *projected* expense for the current period (since it recurs monthly, maybe next rent is due within the period? Period Nov 11–Dec 4; next monthly rent from Nov 13 would be Dec 13, not within). No.\n\nHmm, what if the recurring template's \"next\" date is Dec 13 but the app incorrectly includes it in the current period summary? Then the budget would be over-counting future. Still a bug.\n\nOK, let me try a different reproduction to make the developer see: add a new recurring expense today (e.g., $10 recurring with category Transport) and see if the budget sheet Spent increases by $10 once or twice. If twice, that's a crisp repro for double-counting recurring templates. But that changes data; we can delete it after. But deleting a recurring template might be tricky. Hmm.\n\nActually, even better: use the existing Rent template. If I edit the Rent expense amount from 20 to 30 (via history Edit), then:\n- If double counted: budget sheet spent increases by 20 (from 80 to 100) because both template and transaction updated? Or maybe template stays 20 and transaction 30 → spent 90? Hmm.\n- Analytics would increase by 10 (60→70).\n\nThis could reveal whether the $20 is tied to the template. But editing the Rent expense to 30 changes data; we can revert. Also editing a recurring instance may ask \"this occurrence only\" vs \"all\". Let's try carefully with reversible values. But risky.\n\nAlternatively, delete the recurring Rent expense's *series*? No.\n\nHmm, maybe I should test just the \"recurring templates are now included in the entries the summary is built from\" by creating a new recurring template with no transaction? When you toggle \"Recurrent payment\" on a new expense and submit, it creates a template + transaction. Then budget summary might count the template only if its next date is within the period? Hmm.\n\nLet me step back and think about what \"summary rows\" are. In the budget sheet, there are two summary cards: the orange \"Spent\" card and black \"Total budget\" card, plus the \"Calculated amount\" row. \"Summary rows\" plural could be the rows in \"How it's calculated\": \"Budget remaining\", \"What you spent\", \"Total\". The change says recurring templates are now included in the entries the summary is built from. So the summary's \"What you spent\" / spent total is built from entries, and recurring templates are now included in the entries. This double counts a template that's already materialized as an expense. So y